.TH BABYSIT 1 "2026-11-15" "babysit" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
babysit \- run a command detached, log it, and return early if it stalls
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B babysit
.RI [ options ]
.RB [ \-\- ]
.I command
.RI [ args ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B babysit
runs
.I command
in the background under a babysitter process, echoes its output, and writes
that output to a log file.
By default it waits for the command and returns its exit status.
With the options below it returns earlier: when an expected line appears,
when the command stops producing output, or after a fixed time.
.PP
Returning early never stops the command.
The command keeps running and its output keeps going to the log, so the
caller can come back later to read the log, wait longer, or kill it.
.PP
Three processes are involved:
.TP
.B parent
The
.B babysit
that was invoked.
It waits for the babysitter to say why it should return and turns that into
an exit status.
.TP
.B babysitter
A detached process in its own session.
It reads the command's stdout and stderr, writes the log, echoes the log to
the caller's stdout, and runs the timers.
When the parent should return, it closes its copy of the caller's stdout and
carries on logging.
.TP
.B child
The command.
It gets
.I /dev/null
as stdin, pipes to the babysitter as stdout and stderr, and its own process
group.
It never holds the caller's terminal or pipes, so a caller that waits for
end-of-file on
.BR babysit 's
output is not kept waiting by it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "\-e, \-\-expect " REGEX
Return 0 as soon as an output line matches the POSIX extended regular
expression
.IR REGEX .
The line is matched without its
.B OUT:
or
.B ERR:
prefix; lines from both streams are checked.
.TP
.B \-F, \-\-fixed
Treat the
.B \-\-expect
pattern as a plain substring.
.TP
.BI "\-i, \-\-idle " SECS
Return 125 if the command writes nothing to stdout or stderr for
.IR SECS .
Every byte restarts the timer.
.TP
.BI "\-t, \-\-timeout " SECS
Return 124 once
.I SECS
have passed in total.
.TP
.B \-d, \-\-detach
Return 0 as soon as the command has started.
.TP
.BI "\-l, \-\-log\-dir " DIR
Base log directory.
The default is
.IR $HOME/.local/var/log/babysit .
.TP
.BI "\-\-get\-child\-pid " PID
Run nothing.
Print the pid of the command started during this boot by the
.B babysit
whose pid was
.IR PID ;
see
.BR "FINDING THE COMMAND AGAIN" .
.TP
.BI "\-\-get\-child\-log " PID
Run nothing.
Print the path of that command's log.
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
Show the version.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Show a summary of options.
.PP
.I SECS
may be fractional and may carry an
.BR s ,
.B m
or
.B h
suffix (0.5, 30s, 2m).
.SH LOG FILES
Each command is logged to
.PP
.RS
.IR LOGDIR / boot-time / generation / babysit-pid / child-pid /log
.RE
.PP
where
.I boot-time
is the time the system booted, as
.IR YYYYmmdd-HHMMSS ,
.I generation
is a number that is 0 unless pids have been reused during this boot (see
.BR "PID REUSE" ),
.I babysit-pid
is the pid of the
.B babysit
that was invoked and
.I child-pid
is the pid of the command.
The first is the one the caller already has, as
.B $!
in a shell, and it is enough to find the second; see
.BR "FINDING THE COMMAND AGAIN" .
.PP
The full path is also printed as the first line of output
.RB ( "Log file: ..." ).
.PP
Pids are only meaningful within one boot, which is why each boot has its own
directory.
The directory name is worked out the first time
.B babysit
runs after a boot and remembered in
.IR LOGDIR /.boot ,
so that every run during that boot uses the same one.
Normally only the current boot's directory and one generation are present,
so the wildcards above match exactly one path; earlier boots and superseded
generations are packed away by
.BR babysitd (8).
On systems without a kernel boot id (anything but Linux) all logs go under
.IR LOGDIR /unknown-boot .
The log contains, in order:
.IP \(bu 2
the command line;
.IP \(bu 2
.BI "Process created with pid " N ;
.IP \(bu 2
every line of output, prefixed with
.B "OUT: "
(stdout) or
.B "ERR: "
(stderr);
.IP \(bu 2
.BI "Process exited with code " N
or
.BI "Process killed by signal " N
once the command is gone.
.PP
If the log cannot be written while the command is running, because the disk
has filled up or a file size limit was reached, the command is left running
and its output is still echoed.
.B babysit
prints one warning on its stdout, and if writing works again later the log
gets a line saying how many bytes are missing at that point.
A log that ends without a
.B "Process exited"
or
.B "Process killed"
line is not proof that the command is still running.
.PP
Output that does not end in a newline, such as a prompt, is logged as a line
of its own after one second, or at once when an idle stall is reported.
Lines longer than 4096 bytes are split.
.SH FINDING THE COMMAND AGAIN
A caller that remembers the pid of the
.B babysit
it started can ask for the command's pid and its log:
.PP
.RS
.nf
babysit \-\-expect 'Listening on' \-\-idle 30s \-\- ./server &
ppid=$!
wait "$ppid"; rc=$?

bbid=$(babysit \-\-get\-child\-pid "$ppid")
log=$(babysit \-\-get\-child\-log "$ppid")
.fi
.RE
.PP
Both look only at the current boot and search its generations newest first,
so if that pid has been reused the most recent run is the one reported.
Give the same
.B \-\-log\-dir
as the run did, if it had one.
They print one line and exit 0, or print nothing and exit 1 if that pid has
no log: because nothing was started (exit status 2, 126 or 127), or because
.BR babysitd (8)
has since packed or expired it.
.PP
The log is created by the babysitter just before the command is executed,
so a lookup made straight after starting
.B babysit
in the background can be too early.
Once
.B babysit
has returned, or has printed its first line, the log is there.
.PP
The pid printed says which process the command
.I was.
It does not say the command is still running, and once it has exited the
number can be given to an unrelated process.
Before sending a signal, check that the log does not yet end in
.B "Process exited"
or
.BR "Process killed" .
To stop the command together with its children:
.PP
.RS
kill \-\- \-"$bbid"
.RE
.SH PID REUSE
Log paths are built from pids, and pids are reused.
After a reboot numbering starts again, which is why each boot logs to its own
directory and cannot collide with another.
Within one boot a pid is reused only after the system has run through its
whole pid range, which is rare but possible on a long-lived machine.
.PP
.B babysit
never overwrites or deletes a log to make room.
Within a boot, logs are filed in numbered
.IR generations ,
starting at 0, and new logs always go to the highest-numbered one.
Once the command's pid is known, and before the command is executed, the
babysitter checks that generation for either of the two pids it is about to
use:
.IP \(bu 2
.IR generation / babysit-pid
exists, meaning an earlier
.B babysit
had the same pid; or
.IP \(bu 2
.IR generation /*/ child-pid
exists, meaning an earlier command had the same pid.
.PP
If neither exists the log is created there.
If either does, the pid range has wrapped around.
The old log is left exactly as it is, whether or not it is still being
written, and the new log goes into the next generation, which is created if
need be.
Every later
.B babysit
uses that generation too.
The command is started in all cases; a pid collision is never an error.
.PP
Starting a new generation is also the request to pack the old one.
While a babysitter is logging it holds a write lock on its log file, which
the kernel releases when the babysitter exits for any reason, so a locked
log is one that is still being written.
The babysitter that starts a generation wakes
.BR babysitd (8),
if it is running, which looks for generations that are no longer the highest
and, as soon as none of their logs is locked, replaces the directory
.IR boot-time / N
with
.IR boot-time / N .tar.gz .
Until then the superseded generation stays readable in place, and commands
still running in it keep logging to it.
A packed generation's number is not used again.
.PP
After a reboot,
.BR babysitd (8)
packs the whole of an earlier boot, generations included, into
.IR LOGDIR /archive/babysit_logs_ boot-time .tar.gz ,
and it deletes archives and finished logs that are older than its
configured limit.
Without
.BR babysitd (8)
nothing is packed or deleted; generations and boot directories simply
accumulate.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.I N
The command exited with status
.I N
(128 plus the signal number if it was killed by a signal).
.TP
.B 0
.B \-\-expect
matched, or
.B \-\-detach
was given.
.TP
.B 1
.B \-\-expect
was given but the command exited with status 0 without a matching line.
.TP
.B 124
.B \-\-timeout
expired; the command is still running.
.TP
.B 125
Nothing was written for
.B \-\-idle
seconds; the command is still running.
.TP
.B 126, 127
The command could not be executed, or was not found.
No log is left behind.
.TP
.B 2
Usage or internal error.
Nothing was started.
.PP
With
.B \-\-get\-child\-pid
or
.BR \-\-get\-child\-log :
0 if the log was found, 1 if not.
.PP
The reason for returning is also printed on stderr.
A command that itself exits with one of the special statuses cannot be told
apart by status alone; the last line of the log settles it.
.SH NOTES
The command's output is echoed to the caller's stdout for as long as
.B babysit
has not returned.
If the caller reads it slowly, or stops reading without closing it, the
babysitter waits, and once the pipes in between are full the command waits
too, as it would in any pipeline.
.B \-\-timeout
still expires on time while this is going on, and
.B babysit
then returns and the command carries on, logging only.
Time spent waiting for the caller is not the command's silence and does not
count towards
.BR \-\-idle .
.PP
Killing or interrupting
.B babysit
does not stop the command.
The babysitter notices that its parent is gone, releases the caller's
stdout, and keeps logging.
.PP
If the command exits while its own children still hold its stdout or stderr
open, the exit is reported and logged at once, and their later output is
still appended to the log.
.SH CAVEATS
.B babysit
reports what it can observe from outside: bytes arriving on two pipes, a
line that matches, the clock, an exit status.
It does not know whether the command is healthy, making progress or stuck,
and it never stops the command.
Each of its answers means exactly what is said below and no more; what to do
about it is for the caller to decide.
.SS "\-\-idle means no output, not hung"
A command that is working hard without printing anything is reported as
stalled, and one that is stuck in a loop printing the same line is not.
Status 125 is a reason to go and look, not a verdict.
.PP
Output buffering makes this worse than it sounds.
Most programs flush each line when stdout is a terminal but collect several
kilobytes before writing when it is a pipe, as it is under
.BR babysit .
A program that visibly prints a line a second in a terminal can therefore be
silent for minutes here and deliver everything at once, or only when it
exits.
Where that matters, turn the buffering off in the command:
.B "stdbuf \-oL"
.I command
for programs using C stdio,
.B "python \-u"
or
.BR PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1 ,
and the equivalent for other runtimes.
The same delay applies to
.BR \-\-expect .
.SS "\-\-expect means a line matched, not ready"
The pattern is tried against each line as it is written to the log, and a
match says only that such a line was printed.
A logged line is at most 4096 bytes, and output that stops without a newline
for a second is logged as a line of its own, so a pattern cannot match
across either kind of break.
Text after a NUL byte in a line is not seen.
A prompt that does not end in a newline is matched only once it has been
logged, about a second after it was printed.
.SH EXAMPLES
Start a server and return once it is listening:
.PP
.RS
babysit \-\-expect 'Listening on' \-\-timeout 30 \-\- ./server \-\-port 8080
.RE
.PP
Run a build, but come back if it goes quiet for two minutes:
.PP
.RS
babysit \-\-idle 2m \-\- make \-j8
.RE
.SH \&
We are aware that
.B babysitter
and
.B baby
is a bad metaphor.
We are aware that the babysitter is the parent process, and we are aware
that our docs refer to 'babysitter' and 'child' even though it could have
more internally consistent to use 'babysitter' and 'baby'.
We like it more the way it is.
We will
.I never
change the documentation or example code.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR babysitd (8),
.BR babysit.conf (5),
.BR timeout (1)
